@prefix agp: <https://dagalog.no/ns/agentprov#> .
@prefix bl: <https://dagalog.no/ns/backlog#> .
@prefix ghissues: <https://github.com/daghovland/rdf-datalog/issues/> .
@prefix ghpull: <https://github.com/daghovland/rdf-datalog/pull/> .
@prefix prov: <http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#> .
@prefix rdfs: <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .
@prefix xsd: <http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#> .
@prefix session: <https://dagalog.no/ns/agentprov/session#> .

# PROSPECTIVE summary scaffold, generated by scripts/new-provenance-summary.sh.
# TODO: replace the placeholder summaryText/decisionPoint below with the
# actual distilled reasoning behind this PR before committing — see
# docs/plans/TRANSCRIPT_SUMMARY_GUIDELINES.md. Timestamps below are real
# (from `git log main..HEAD` on branch feat/444-provenance-readability); everything else that
# isn't marked TODO can be left as-is.

ghpull:458 a bl:PullRequest, bl:WorkItem ;
    rdfs:label "backlog dashboard: improve Provenance/summary readability (#444)" ;
    bl:number 458 ;
    bl:state bl:Open ;
    bl:closesIssue ghissues:444 .

session:claudeSonnet5 a prov:SoftwareAgent ;
    rdfs:label "Claude Sonnet 5" .

session:pr458 a agp:AgentSession ;
    prov:wasAssociatedWith session:claudeSonnet5 ;
    prov:used ghissues:444 ;
    prov:startedAtTime "2026-08-12T17:55:28Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-08-12T17:55:28Z"^^xsd:dateTime .

session:pr458Summary a agp:TranscriptSummary ;
    agp:summaryText "Issue #444 asked for readability improvements to agp:summaryText/agp:abstractText prose rendered in the backlog dashboard: these strings are often 500-2000+ characters of dense single-paragraph reasoning (e.g. pr-434.ttl's 1597-char summaryText), but were rendering full-width in the same tight line-height as short structured UI labels around them, across three call sites (Provenance tab, item detail page, and the \"what's relevant\" panel, the last of which even had its own bespoke .relevant-pr-text CSS rule and a BIND(COALESCE(abstractText, summaryText)) SPARQL shortcut that silently dropped whichever field wasn't picked). Applied a narrower measure (max-width: 62ch) and roomier line-height/font-size than the surrounding chrome, and, where abstractText exists, show it prominently with the full summaryText collapsed behind a <details> \"Full reasoning\" disclosure so the abstract orients the reader without pushing the full text into view by default; when abstractText is absent (older summaries), summaryText renders directly with no wrapping <details>. Extracted one shared renderSummaryText(container, abstractText, summaryText) helper used by all three sites instead of three separate implementations, and changed the relevant-panel's SPARQL query to select summaryText/abstractText as separate bindings so the helper can see both. Verified against the real corpus via scripts/serve-backlog.sh + curl against the live SPARQL endpoint rather than trusting the code alone: confirmed pr-434.ttl (no abstractText) exercises the fallback path and several summaries (pr-300, pr-328, pr-344, pr-348, pr-349) exercise the abstract-prominent/collapsed-details path." ;
    agp:reasoningFor ghpull:458 ;
    agp:decisionPoint session:pr458Decision ;
    prov:wasGeneratedBy session:pr458 ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo session:claudeSonnet5 .

session:pr458Decision a agp:Decision ;
    agp:summaryText "Deliberately did not add a render-time paragraph-splitting heuristic to break the long prose into multiple paragraphs. The source summaryText/abstractText strings have no embedded structure (no blank lines, no markdown) to key a split off of, and they are genuinely single continuous arguments rather than several distinct points -- a sentence- or length-based splitting guess would look arbitrary and could break mid-thought. Chose the abstractText-prominent/collapsed-summaryText disclosure structure instead, which uses the data's own two-tier structure (short abstract vs. long full text) rather than inventing one." ;
    agp:alternative "Considered always showing the full summaryText inline with only the narrower-measure/line-height typography change (no collapsing disclosure). Rejected because on the Provenance tab and item detail page, several summaries in a list would each contribute 1500+ characters of visible text, pushing everything below far down the page; the collapsed-details structure keeps a scannable list while still making the full reasoning one click away." .
